A||||11||Gokart||No.1 spark plug?||||5||
Z||000000||Gokart||01-01-2000||11:15 PM||||Sorry, I thought I was done bothering you all tonight but I got the timing set and it's still not running "just right"! I know it must have something to do with no.1 plug/wire. It's the only one that I can remove and the engine is not affected one bit. Wire checks out on my ohm meter and I know the plugs ok. Any ideas? I'll probably get it running perfect right before a big snow storm and I'll have to go back to my jap crap tercel!||||reg||5
Z||000001||fiero56||01-01-2000||11:25 PM||||That's pretty interesting. Try this: pull the fuel injector fuse from the fuse panel, start the engine, it will run for a second or so then die. now pull the #1 sparkplug, hook the plug wire up to dist. cap and plug. Have someone crank the engine while you hold the plug about 1/4" from grounded bare steel. You should see an arc going from the plug to the steel.  If not, then power isn't getting through the plug for some reason. Could be: bad plug, wire(even though it checked out), maybe your cap is doing something funky for #1 plug wire. This is my best guess if the problem is with #1 cylinder ignition.<p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 01-02-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||cobra427||01-01-2000||11:40 PM||||hey neighbor, did you get my E=Mail  yet?check ok||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||01-02-2000||12:08 AM||||Replace the plugs, wires, cap and rotor. It's fairly cheap, and then you know you have good parts. A wire that tests good on a meter doesn't mean a thing. 25+Kv will find even the smallest breach to jump to ground. The only way to test the wires and cap is with a megger box. This box tests the insulation. It's expensive. It's far cheaper to just replace the suspect parts.<P>Don't worry about what plug is "best" either. None of the "specialty" plugs are having an easy time right now showing they are really better than a good brand of basic plug. In fact if you motor uses any oil at all the basic plugs often work better, and are cheap enough to change every year or 2 without making you feel like you put a bunch of cash to waste. ((I just replaced my specialty plugs with basic Autolite and got the same result as when I put in the specialty ones. for about 4$us instead of 20.)<P>Compression test the motor. you may have toasted a valve or ring.<P>if the cyl is running at all. then it could also be crap in the injector. try running some cleaner thru. One of the cleaner kits that attaches to the fuel rail should do it, but even something in the tank can make a diferance. It's posible that #1's injector is failing, but clean anyway just to make sure it's not dirt.||||reg||1
Z||000004||fiero56||01-02-2000||12:53 AM||||Ogre,<BR>It's a good thing your around. My knowledge only goes so far, yours seems limitless. You must be saying: "there goes Drew again, thinking he knows everything, just making peoples problems worse, and confusing them even more." I hope I'm helpful to at least a couple of people now and then. <BR>If not, I'll go sulk, then have someone do something to my car, then I can go play and diagnose the problem. And I can give myself my own bad advice.<BR>I thought Gokart mentioned he had replaced cap, wires, plugs. Maybe I read another post thinking it was his.<P>By the way, whats a megger box? I've never heard of them.<p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 01-02-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||geyser||01-02-2000||01:40 AM||||I tested mine with a megger box and now I have to get a shot.||||reg||1
